From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gregory Mitchell <me@gmitch215.xyz>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 16:21:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] mm/page_alloc: yield to the other cpus after an OOM kill

The allocator retries after the OOM killer has picked a victim and
leaves it to run and exit on another cpu. Every cpu shares one host
thread here, so the retrying task never gave the victim the thread:
at 1,200 pages the kernel logged its OOM kills and then spun at 100%
cpu in try_to_free_pages and out_of_memory until the host stopped it.
The retry now calls cpu_relax, which yields to the other cpus.
---
 mm/page_alloc.c | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/mm/page_alloc.c b/mm/page_alloc.c
index 2d4b6f1a5..33bb60dec 100644
--- a/mm/page_alloc.c
+++ b/mm/page_alloc.c
@@ -4947,6 +4947,8 @@ __alloc_pages_slowpath(gfp_t gfp_mask, unsigned int order,
 	/* Retry as long as the OOM killer is making progress */
 	if (did_some_progress) {
 		no_progress_loops = 0;
+		/* the cpus share one host thread: yield so the victim can run and exit */
+		cpu_relax();
 		goto retry;
 	}
 
